.TH NVDA-RDP-SERVER 7 2026-11-26 "nvda-rdp-linux 0.5.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
nvda-rdp-server \- Orca's speech and braille through the NVDA of an RDP client
.SH DESCRIPTION
With
.BR nvda-rdp-server ,
a blind user on Windows, with NVDA and its RDAccess add-on, who connects with
RDP to GNOME Remote Desktop's remote login, hears Orca through NVDA's
synthesizer and reads Orca's braille on NVDA's display. The keys of the
display and braille typing go back to Orca. Nothing changes on the Windows
side, and nothing changes for local sessions of the Linux computer: their Orca
keeps the usual Speech Dispatcher and BRLTTY.
.PP
It needs GNOME 49 or later: GDM's login screens of remote connections run as
their own users, and Orca runs as the systemd user service
.BR orca.service .
.SS How it works
.TP
.B libnvda-rdp-grd-preload.so
A library loaded with
.B LD_PRELOAD
into GNOME Remote Desktop's session daemon
.RB ( gnome-remote-desktop-handover.service ).
In each RDP connection it opens the
.B NVDA-SPEECH
and
.B NVDA-BRAILLE
dynamic virtual channels and presents them as the Unix sockets
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gnome-remote-desktop/nvda-speech.sock
and
.IR nvda-braille.sock .
GNOME Remote Desktop itself is not modified.
.TP
.B nvda-rdp-server-session.service
At the start of each graphical session, including GDM's login screen, decides
from logind's
.B Remote
and
.B Class
properties whether the session is remote. For a remote session it writes the
environment Orca needs; otherwise it changes nothing.
.TP
.B nvda-rdp-speechd.service
In remote sessions, a Speech Dispatcher instance of the session whose output
module sends Orca's speech to NVDA, with indexes, interruption and Orca's
voice changes as NVDA's relative rate, pitch and volume commands. While NVDA
is not attached, the messages go to the session's usual Speech Dispatcher,
heard through RDP audio.
.TP
.B nvda-rdp-brlapi.service
In remote sessions, a BrlAPI server of the session that presents NVDA's
braille display, at its size, to Orca. It writes Orca's text into dots with
liblouis and Orca's table, and turns the display's keys into Orca's commands.
.TP
.B orca.service
A drop-in gives Orca the environment written for remote sessions
.RB ( SPEECHD_ADDRESS ,
.BR BRLAPI_HOST ,
.BR BRLAPI_AUTH )
and starts it after the two servers.
.PP
All of them are user units of every user, GDM's login screen users included.
They need not be enabled: the drop-in of
.B orca.service
pulls them in, and the two servers start only in remote sessions.
.SH CONFIGURATION
GDM's remote login must be enabled in GNOME Remote Desktop's system daemon,
in GNOME Settings (System, Remote Desktop, Remote Login), or as root:
.PP
.RS
.nf
grdctl \-\-system rdp set\-tls\-key /var/lib/gnome\-remote\-desktop/tls/tls.key
grdctl \-\-system rdp set\-tls\-cert /var/lib/gnome\-remote\-desktop/tls/tls.crt
grdctl \-\-system rdp set\-credentials USER
grdctl \-\-system rdp enable
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B set-credentials
asks for the password. These are the credentials of the RDP connection to the
login screen, not those of a user account.
.PP
For Orca to speak on the login screen, the screen reader must be enabled
there: from the accessibility menu of the login screen, or in GDM's dconf
settings, key
.B screen-reader-enabled
of
.BR org.gnome.desktop.a11y.applications .
.TP
.B NVDA_RDP_LOGIN_SCREEN
Set to
.B no
in
.B nvda-rdp-server-session.service
to leave the login screen of remote connections with its usual output; only
the users' sessions are then served. For every user, with a drop-in such as
.IR /etc/systemd/user/nvda-rdp-server-session.service.d/login-screen.conf :
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Service]
Environment=NVDA_RDP_LOGIN_SCREEN=no
.fi
.RE
.PP
On NixOS the module option
.B services.nvda-rdp-server.loginScreen
does this.
.TP
.B NVDA_RDP_PROSODY
Set to
.B 0
in
.B nvda-rdp-speechd.service
to stop sending Orca's voice changes to NVDA.
.TP
.B NVDA_RDP_LOG_LEVEL
Level of the speech module's log, set in
.BR nvda-rdp-speechd.service :
.B DEBUG
includes the spoken text. Default:
.BR INFO .
.TP
.B WLOG_FILTER
Set to
.B nvda-rdp.grd-preload:DEBUG
in
.B gnome-remote-desktop-handover.service
to see the channel library's lines in the journal; GNOME Remote Desktop logs
only warnings by default.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nvda-rdp-server/
State of the session:
.I remote
when it is served, and
.IR orca.env .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nvda-rdp-brlapi.key
Key of the session's BrlAPI server, written at each start, mode 0600.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nvda-rdp-speechd-module.log
Log of the speech module.
.TP
.I /usr/share/nvda-rdp-linux/speechd/speechd.conf
Configuration of the session's Speech Dispatcher.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The programs log to the journal under their own names:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl \-b \-t nvda\-rdp\-server\-session \-t nvda\-rdp\-brlapi\-server \e
  \-t gnome\-remote\-desktop\-daemon
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B nvda-rdp-server-session
logs one line per session, for example
.B "remote session: Orca uses the nvda-rdp BrlAPI server and Speech Dispatcher"
or
.BR "local session: Orca keeps its usual braille and speech" .
Once NVDA is attached,
.B "NVDA-BRAILLE: protocol version 2 negotiated"
is in the journal and
.B "NVDA-SPEECH: protocol version 2 negotiated"
in the speech module's log.
.SH SECURITY
The sockets of the channels are in a directory of mode 0700, with mode 0600.
The BrlAPI server listens on 127.0.0.1 only, port 4101 plus the user's UID,
and accepts only clients that know the key of the session.
The speech and braille are what Orca presents and are presumed confidential:
the logs contain them only at the
.B DEBUG
level.
Any program of the remote session can write to the NVDA channels, as on
Windows with RDAccess.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The channel library depends on internal details of GNOME Remote Desktop (one
.B WTSOpenServerA
and one
.B WTSCloseServer
per connection); a new version can break it. It serves GDM's remote login
only, not the sharing of a local session or headless sessions.
.PP
An account has one
.BR orca.service :
if it has a local and a remote session at the same time, the first one opened
decides. A new remote login to the same account joins its existing session.
.PP
Only the differences from Orca's default voice reach NVDA; Orca's choice of
synthesizer has no effect there. Orca's choice of voice changes NVDA's voice,
and
.BR nvda\-rdp\-settings (1)
changes NVDA's driver settings, only if driver settings management is allowed
in RDAccess on the client. A change of the display size costs
a reconnection of about 2 seconds.
.PP
Caps Lock is not synchronized between the remote session and Windows.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nvda\-rdp\-client (1),
.BR nvda\-rdp\-settings (1),
.BR grdctl (1),
.BR orca (1),
.BR speech\-dispatcher (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5)
.PP
